---
import CalculatorGuideArticle from '../../components/CalculatorGuideArticle.astro';
---

<CalculatorGuideArticle
  toolSlug="triangle-calculator"
  label="Triangle calculator guide"
  title="How to use the Triangle Calculator"
  description="Learn how to calculate triangle area, perimeter, angles, and triangle type from three side lengths."
  path="/blog/how-to-use-triangle-calculator/"
  intro="The Triangle Calculator takes three side lengths and turns them into area, perimeter, angles, and triangle classification."
  quickStart={[
    'Enter side a, side b, and side c using the same unit.',
    'Add a unit label if you want the result to show cm, m, in, or another unit.',
    'Press Calculate triangle.',
    'Review area, perimeter, semiperimeter, angles, side type, and angle type.',
  ]}
  sections={[
    {
      title: 'When to use three-side triangle mode',
      paragraphs: [
        'Use this calculator when you know all three side lengths but do not know the height. The calculator first checks that the sides can form a real triangle.',
        'After the sides pass the triangle inequality check, it uses Heron\'s formula for area and the law of cosines for angle estimates.',
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Reading the answer',
      paragraphs: [
        'Area is shown in square units. Perimeter and semiperimeter are shown in the same length unit you entered.',
        'The classification helps you spot whether the triangle is scalene, isosceles, equilateral, acute, right, or obtuse.',
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Common mistakes to avoid',
      paragraphs: [
        'Do not mix units in the same calculation. Convert all sides first if one side is in inches and another is in centimeters.',
        'If the calculator says the triangle inequality fails, the three side lengths cannot close into a triangle.',
      ],
    },
  ]}
  sidecarText="Use the Triangle Calculator while you follow the guide."
/>
